              %path{:d => "M 130.21425 23.356938 L 185.52675 -19.377437 ", :fill => "none", :stroke => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "stroke-linecap" => "butt", "stroke-linejoin" => "miter", "stroke-miterlimit" => "10", "stroke-opacity" => "1", "stroke-width" => "0.47818", :transform => "matrix(1, 0, 0, -1, 206.692, 39.896)"}
              %path{:d => "M -2.486181 2.867808 C -2.032661 1.149789 -1.017725 0.334467 0.000490904 0.000519846 C -1.019901 -0.333641 -2.031912 -1.145103 -2.487048 -2.869071 ", :fill => "none", :stroke => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "stroke-linecap" => "round", "stroke-linejoin" => "round", "stroke-miterlimit" => "10", "stroke-opacity" => "1", "stroke-width" => "0.47818", :transform => "matrix(0.7913, 0.61133, 0.61133, -0.7913, 392.40945, 59.41808)"}
              %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                %use{:x => "368.267", "xlink:href" => "#uIOkUGlRREaZuF59t_ZsGLnTT2A=-glyph-4-1", :y => "32.598"}
              %path{:d => "M -185.262313 -27.783687 L -130.047469 -27.783687 ", :fill => "none", :stroke => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "stroke-linecap" => "butt", "stroke-linejoin" => "miter", "stroke-miterlimit" => "10", "stroke-opacity" => "1", "stroke-width" => "0.47818", :transform => "matrix(1, 0, 0, -1, 206.692, 39.896)"}
              %path{:d => "M -2.487399 2.86907 C -2.030368 1.146414 -1.018649 0.333914 0.0008825 0.0018825 C -1.018649 -0.334055 -2.030368 -1.146555 -2.487399 -2.869211 ", :fill => "none", :stroke => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "stroke-linecap" => "round", "stroke-linejoin" => "round", "stroke-miterlimit" => "10", "stroke-opacity" => "1", "stroke-width" => "0.47818", :transform => "matrix(1, 0, 0, -1, 76.88193, 67.68157)"}
              %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                %use{:x => "46.186", "xlink:href" => "#uIOkUGlRREaZuF59t_ZsGLnTT2A=-glyph-4-1", :y => "62.228"}
          %p Now let
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                be any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo ,
                      %mi G
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F, G \,\colon\, C \rightarrow K
                be
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
                such that every map in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is sent to a map in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                possessing a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
                and satisfying the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beck-Chevalley+Condition"} Beck-Chevalley Condition
                for any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+square"} commutative square
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi F
                      %mo →
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \,\colon\, F \rightarrow G
                be a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
          %p Then:
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (universal property of the bicategory of spans)
              %br/
              The following holds:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi η
                          %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_C
                  is
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal"} universal
                  among such functors
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi F
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  as above factors as
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo =
                        %mover
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mo ∘
                        %msub
                          %mi η
                          %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = \hat{F} \circ \eta_C
                  for a functor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mi Span
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{F} \,\colon\, Span_2(C) \rightarrow K
                  which is unique up to isomorphism.
              %li
                %p
                  There exists a unique
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+natural+transformation"} lax natural transformation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi α
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mover
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mo →
                        %mover
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{\alpha} \,\colon\, \hat{F} \rightarrow \hat{G}
                  such that
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mover
                            %mi α
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                          %msub
                            %mi η
                            %mi C
                          %mo =
                          %mi α
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{\alpha} \eta_C = \alpha
              %li
                %p
                  Let
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y
                  be objects in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %mi x
                        %mo →
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: x \rightarrow y
                  be a morphism in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi C
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi α
                          %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi α
                          %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\alpha_x, \alpha_y)
                  induce a pseudo-map of adjoints
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⊣
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Ff
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo *
                          %mo →
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⊣
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Gf
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo *
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(f) \dashv (Ff)^* \rightarrow G(f) \dashv (Gf)^*
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi α
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{\alpha}
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudonatural+transformation"} pseudonatural transformation
            %p
              Furthermore, if we denote
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Pbk
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pbk
              as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
              of categories with pullbacks,
              = succeed "-preserving" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              = succeed "s," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equifibered+natural+transformation"} equifibered natural transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi BiCat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BiCat
              as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tricategory"} tricategory
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategories"} bicategories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Span
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi Pbk
                    %mo →
                    %mi BiCat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Span(-): Pbk \rightarrow BiCat
              is well-defined as a functor.
          This is due to
          = succeed "." do
            %a{:href => "#Hermida99"} Hermida 1999
          %h3#LimitsAndColimits Limits and colimits
          %p
            Since a category of spans/correspondences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Corr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Corr(\mathcal{C})
            is evidently
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            to its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            exists they are also
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            and vice versa.
          %p
            If the underlying category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+category"} extensive category
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Corr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Corr(\mathcal{C})
            is given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/32526/what-is-the-product-in-the-2-category-of-spans"} this
            MO discussion).
          %p
            More generally, every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/van+Kampen+colimit"} van Kampen colimit
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is a (co)limit in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Corr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Corr(\mathcal{C})
            — and conversely, this property characterizes van Kampen colimits. (
            %a{:href => "SobocinskiHeindel11"}> Sobocinski-Heindel 11
            ).
          %h3#closure Closure
          %p
            When
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Span
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Span(E)
            is a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+bicategory"} closed bicategory
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Spans in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinSet"} FinSet
                behave like the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrices"} matrices
                with entries in the
                = succeed "s:" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ←
                      %mi N
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1 \leftarrow N \to X_2
                a span of finite sets, the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi x
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi x
                            %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{x_1, x_2}
                over any two elements
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1 \in X_1
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_2 \in X_2
                plays the role of the corresponding matrix entry. Under this identification composition of spans indeed corresponds to matrix multiplication. This implies that the category of spans of finite sets is equivalent to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
                of commutative monoids, that is, to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PROP"} PROP
                for the free bicommutative
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bialgebra"} bialgebra
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rig+category"} rig category
                with respect to the tensor products induced by the coproduct and product in FinSet. The coproduct in FinSet remains the coproduct, but the product becomes the bilinear
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+modules"} tensor product of modules
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Burnside+category"} Burnside category
                is essentially the category of correspondences in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-sets"} G-sets
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite group
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi in
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{in}
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi out
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{out}
                is an example of a cospan
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi in
                      %mo →
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo ←
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi out
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{in} \to \Sigma \leftarrow \Sigma_{out}
                in the category of
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
                again some extra technical aspects must be added (“
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/collars"}> collars
                ”).
            %li
              %p
                In
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum field theory
                (see there for details), spans of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"} stacks
                model
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectories"} trajectories
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+Chow+motives"} category of Chow motives
                has as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+classes"} equivalence classes
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+combinations"} linear combinations
                of spans of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+variety"} smooth
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+varieties"} projective varieties
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weinstein+symplectic+category"} Weinstein symplectic category
                has as morphisms
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+correspondences"} Lagrangian correspondences
                between
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic manifolds
              %p
                More generally
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+dual+pairs"} symplectic dual pairs
                are correspondences between
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifolds"} Poisson manifolds
            %li
              %p
                Cospans of homomorphisms of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
                represent morphisms in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
                (by Cuntz’ result).
            %li
              %p
                Correspondences of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flag+manifolds"} flag manifolds
                play a role as
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twistor+correspondences"} twistor correspondences
                %em
                  %a{:href => "Schubert+calculus#Correspondences"} Schubert calculus – Correspondences
                and at
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horocycle+correspondence"} horocycle correspondence
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier-Mukai+transform"} Fourier-Mukai transform
                is a pull-push operation through correspondences equipped with objects in a cocycle given by an object in a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category+of+quasi-coherent+sheaves"} derived category of quasi-coherent sheaves
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hecke+correspondence"} Hecke correspondence
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypergraph"} hypergraph
                is a span from a set of vertices to a set of (hyper)edges.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category+of+spans"}> (∞,n)-category of spans
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sink"}> sink
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosink"} cosink
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"}> product
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multispan"} multispan
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence+type"} correspondence type
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+graph"} reflexive graph
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horn"} horn
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"}> span
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospan"} cospan
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composable+pair"} composable pair
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composite"} composite
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+triangle"} commutative triangle
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
          %p
            A category of correspondences is a refinement of a category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rel"} Rel
            of relations. See there for more.
          %div
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+structure"} walking structure
                %ul
                  %li
                    %p
                      the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boolean+domain"} boolean domain
                      = succeed ";" do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mn 2
                              %mo ≔
                              %mo ∂
                              %msup
                                %mi Δ
                                %mn 1
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \coloneqq \partial \Delta^1
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair"} pair
                      of
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                  %li
                    %p
                      the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+interval+category"} directed interval category
                      = succeed ";" do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mi I
                              %mo ≔
                              %msup
                                %mi Δ
                                %mn 1
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \coloneqq \Delta^1
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
                  %li
                    %p
                      the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C0%29-horn+category"} (2,0)-horn category
                      = succeed ";" do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %msubsup
                                %mi Λ
                                %mn 0
                                %mn 2
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda_0^2
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospan"} cospan
                  %li
                    %p
                      the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-horn+category"} (2,1)-horn category
                      = succeed ";" do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %msubsup
                                %mi Λ
                                %mn 1
                                %mn 2
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda_1^2
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composable+pair"} composable pair
                  %li
                    %p
                      the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C2%29-horn+category"} (2,2)-horn category
                      = succeed ";" do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %msubsup
                                %mi Λ
                                %mn 2
                                %mn 2
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda_2^2
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
                  %li
                    %p
                      the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-simplex+category"} 2-simplex category
                      = succeed ";" do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi Δ
                                %mn 2
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^2
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+triangle"} commutative triangle
                  %li
                    %p
                      the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+parallel+pair"} walking parallel pair
                  %li
                    %p
                      the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+isomorphism"} walking isomorphism
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adj"}> Adj
                      ; i.e. the walking
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
                  %li
                    %p
                      the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+equivalence"} walking equivalence
                  %li
                    %p
                      the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+adjoint+equivalence"} walking adjoint equivalence
                  %li
                    %p
                      the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+2-isomorphism+with+trivial+boundary"} walking 2-isomorphism with trivial boundary
                  %li
                    %p
                      the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
                      of a
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
                      = succeed ";" do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mi T
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                      = succeed "-" do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mi T
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
          %h2#references References
          %p The terminology “span” appears on page 533 of:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nobuo+Yoneda"}> Nobuo Yoneda
              ,
              %em On ext and exact sequences
              (PhD thesis), Journal of the Faculty of Science, Section I.
              %strong 8
              University of Tokyo (1960) 507–576 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://alpha.math.uga.edu/~lorenz/YonedaExtExactSequences.pdf"}> pdf
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://ci.nii.ac.jp/naid/500000325773"} CiNii:naid/500000325773
          %p
            The
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Span
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Span(C)
            construction was introduced by Jean Bénabou (as an example of a bicategory) in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+B%C3%A9nabou"}> Jean Bénabou
              , Introduction to Bicategories,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lecture Notes in Mathematics 47
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BFb0074299"} doi
          %p Bénabou cites an article by Yoneda (1954) for introducing the concept of span (in the category of categories).
          %p
            An exposition discussing the role of spans in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+theory"} quantum theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              %em Spans in quantum Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/span/"}> web
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/span/span.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2007/10/spans_in_quantum_theory.html"} blog
          %p
            The relationship between spans and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodules"} bimodules
            is briefly discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              %em Bimodules versus spans
              (
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2008/08/bimodules_versus_spans.html"}> blog
              )
          %p
            The relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/van+Kampen+colimits"} van Kampen colimits
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#SobocinskiHeindel11
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pawel+Sobocinski"}> Pawel Sobocinski
              , Tobias Heindel,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Being Van Kampen is a universal property
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1101.4594"} arXiv:1101.4594
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            of categories of spans is due to
          %ul
            %li#Hermida99
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claudio+Hermida"}> Claudio Hermida
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Representable Multicategories
              %strong 151
              (2000), No. 2, 164-225, (
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1006/aima.1999.1877"}> doi:10.1006/aima.1999.1877
              )
          %p and further discussed in:
          %ul
            %li#DawsonParePronk04
              %p
                R. Dawson,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Par%C3%A9"} Robert Paré
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dorette+Pronk"} Dorette Pronk
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Universal properties of Span
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 13
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/13/4/13-04abs.html"} TAC
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2116323"} MR2005m:18002
            %li#DawsonParePronk10
              %p
                R. Dawson,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Par%C3%A9"} Robert Paré
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dorette+Pronk"} Dorette Pronk
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The span construction
                %strong 24
                (2010), No. 13, 302–377,
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/24/13/24-13abs.html"} TAC
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2720187"} MR2720187
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord>
                  Charles Walker
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Charles+Walker"} ?
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Universal properties of bicategories of polynomials
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord>
                  Charles Walker
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Charles+Walker"} ?
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Bicategories of spans as generic bicategories
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.10334"} arXiv:2002.10334
                (2020).
          %p See also Theorem 5.2.1 and 5.3.7 of:
          %ul
            %li
              Paul Balmer, and Ivo Dell’Ambrogio.
              = succeed "." do
                %em Mackey 2-functors and Mackey 2-motives
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.04902"} arXiv:1808.04902
              (2018).
          %p
            The structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-tuply+monoidal+%28n%2Cr%29-category"} monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tricategory"} tricategory
            on spans in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-categories"} 2-categories
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Hoffnung
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Hoffnung"}> Alex Hoffnung
              ,
              %em Spans in 2-Categories: A monoidal tricategory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1112.0560"}> arXiv:1112.0560
              )
          %p
            Generally, an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category+of+spans"} (∞,n)-category of spans
            is indicated in section 3.2 of
          %ul
            %li#Lurie
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Classification+of+Topological+Field+Theories"} On the Classification of Topological Field Theories
